1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,691
Who creates your Java objects?

2
00:00:01,891 --> 00:00:03,040
Without Spring, your code

3
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:04,860
creates a repository,

4
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,907
then passes it into a service.

5
00:00:07,147 --> 00:00:10,080
With Spring, you register bean definitions.

6
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,660
The container uses them to create

7
00:00:11,660 --> 00:00:12,640
managed objects

8
00:00:12,640 --> 00:00:15,100
and wire their dependencies.

9
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:18,300
Here, the service bean asks for a repository.

10
00:00:18,300 --> 00:00:19,923
Spring supplies that bean.

11
00:00:20,163 --> 00:00:21,900
Your application calls getBean

12
00:00:21,900 --> 00:00:24,180
to request the ready service.

13
00:00:24,180 --> 00:00:25,720
In our Java run, its repository

14
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,991
is the same bean from the container.

15
00:00:29,251 --> 00:00:30,620
Here’s the whole flow.

16
00:00:30,620 --> 00:00:32,120
Define your beans.

17
00:00:32,120 --> 00:00:32,980
Spring creates them

18
00:00:32,980 --> 00:00:35,140
and wires their dependencies.

19
00:00:35,140 --> 00:00:36,160
Your application requests

20
00:00:36,160 --> 00:00:37,680
the ready service.

21
00:00:37,680 --> 00:00:38,580
That’s inversion of control—

22
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:39,980
for managed beans,

23
00:00:39,980 --> 00:00:42,368
not every Java object.

